    content: "# Double Number Lines\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 1 of 11 · Ratios · Lesson 4*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** model equivalent ratios on double number lines.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Model equivalent ratios on double number lines.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 1 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Double Number Lines** — sits inside ratios. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a Saturday soccer tournament. Related quantities show up as 5 and 8 (and sometimes a third measure 29).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 5 and 8. One useful related value is 13; another is a scaled pair such as 10 to 16 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a Saturday soccer tournament?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 5 to 8. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 18, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Double Number Lines” but switch the context to a science fair supply run. Use numbers 7, 24, and 29.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** dropping a negative sign when comparing signed numbers.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nRatios grow from earlier fraction sense: comparing parts and wholes, and noticing multiplicative (not only additive) relationships.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 5 and 8. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 5 and 8 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 7 and 29. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a Saturday soccer tournament).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “40.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 7, 4, and 18 in a short story about a Prosper Prep basketball practice. Show work.\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 5, 12, and 35 in a short story about an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Show work.\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 7, 10, and 16 in a short story about a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Show work.\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 14, 10, and 33 in a short story about a band concert ticket table. Show work.\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 7, 9, and 14 in a short story about a Saturday soccer tournament. Show work.\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Double Number Lines” with quantities 14, 9, and 31 in a short story about a science fair supply run. Show work.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 4, or 18. Teacher checks method + units.\n2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 5, 12, or 35. Teacher checks method + units.\n3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 10, or 16. Teacher checks method + units.\n4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 10, or 33. Teacher checks method + units.\n5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 7, 9, or 14. Teacher checks method + units.\n6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 9, or 31. Teacher checks method + units.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Double Number Lines” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to dropping a negative sign when comparing…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Double Number Lines.” Use one concrete numeric example with 5 and 8. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
