    content: "# Tax, Tip, and Discount\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 3 of 11 · Rates and Percentages · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve everyday money percent problems carefully.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 11/4 of 36, or 00% of 36? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Tax, Tip, and Discount** — sits inside rates and percentages. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Related quantities show up as 11 and 4 (and sometimes a third measure 36).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 11 and 4. One useful related value is 15; another is a scaled pair such as 22 to 8 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for an East Texas trail hike near Tyler?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 11 to 4. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 99, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Tax, Tip, and Discount” but switch the context to a church youth group picnic. Use numbers 0, 11, and 36.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume (filling).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPercents are rates “per 100.” Unit rates from earlier lessons transfer directly.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 11 and 4. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 11 and 4 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 0 and 36. Explain why your representation matches the situation (an East Texas trail hike near Tyler).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “44.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 4, 3, and 45 in a short story about a band concert ticket table. Show work.\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 4, -2, and 24 in a short story about a church youth group picnic. Show work.\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 13, 2, and 43 in a short story about a science fair supply run. Show work.\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 13, 5, and 22 in a short story about a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Show work.\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 13, 8, and 41 in a short story about an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Show work.\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Tax, Tip, and Discount” with quantities 13, 4, and 20 in a short story about a school garden plot. Show work.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 4, 3, or 45. Teacher checks method + units.\n2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 4, -2, or 24. Teacher checks method + units.\n3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 2, or 43. Teacher checks method + units.\n4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 5, or 22. Teacher checks method + units.\n5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 8, or 41. Teacher checks method + units.\n6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 4, or 20. Teacher checks method + units.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Tax, Tip, and Discount” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Tax, Tip, and Discount.” Use one concrete numeric example with 11 and 4. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
