    objectives: "• Mixed one-step equations and inequality practice.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Equations & Inequalities Unit Review\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 7 of 11 · Equations & Inequalities · Lesson 10*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** mixed one-step equations and inequality practice.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 6/9 of 32, or 60% of 32? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 7 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Equations & Inequalities Unit Review** — sits inside equations & inequalities. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Related quantities show up as 6 and 9 (and sometimes a third measure 32).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 6 and 9. One useful related value is 15; another is a scaled pair such as 12 to 18 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for an East Texas trail hike near Tyler?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 6 to 9. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 21, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” but switch the context to a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Use numbers 6, 7, and 32.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating percent as 'per ten' instead of 'per hundred'.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nExpressions name patterns; equations assert balance. Keep that distinction sharp.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 6 and 9. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 6 and 9 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 6 and 32. Explain why your representation matches the situation (an East Texas trail hike near Tyler).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “54.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 3, -3, and 17 in a short story about a family road trip on I-20. Show work.\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 12, 1, and 36 in a short story about a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Show work.\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 12, 4, and 15 in a short story about a band concert ticket table. Show work.\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 11, 5, and 42 in a short story about a school garden plot. Show work.\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 11, 8, and 21 in a short story about a family road trip on I-20. Show work.\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” with quantities 11, 3, and 40 in a short story about a Saturday soccer tournament. Show work.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 3, -3, or 17. Teacher checks method + units.\n2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 1, or 36. Teacher checks method + units.\n3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 4, or 15. Teacher checks method + units.\n4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 11, 5, or 42. Teacher checks method + units.\n5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 11, 8, or 21. Teacher checks method + units.\n6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 11, 3, or 40. Teacher checks method + units.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to treating percent as 'per ten' instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Equations & Inequalities Unit Review.” Use one concrete numeric example with 6 and 9. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
