    id: "ppg6m52705b5ecd9b4a0bf4cc",
    title: "Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms",
    description: "Use base × height with perpendicular height.",
    objectives: "• Use base × height with perpendicular height.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 8 of 11 · Plane Figures · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use base × height with perpendicular height.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Use base × height with perpendicular height.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 8 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms** — sits inside plane figures. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a band concert ticket table. Related quantities show up as 8 and -3 (and sometimes a third measure 40).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 8 and -3. One useful related value is 5; another is a scaled pair such as 16 to -6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a band concert ticket table?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 8 to -3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -107, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” but switch the context to a church youth group picnic. Use numbers 4, 15, and 40.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** forgetting that height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGeometry measures need correct units and clear diagrams. Algebraic expressions often summarize geometric patterns.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 8 and -3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 8 and -3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 4 and 40. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a band concert ticket table).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-24.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 4, 1, and 17 in a short story about a library reading challenge. Show work.\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 4, 5, and 36 in a short story about a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Show work.\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 13, 0, and 15 in a short story about an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Show work.\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 12, 1, and 42 in a short story about a school garden plot. Show work.\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 12, 4, and 21 in a short story about a family road trip on I-20. Show work.\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” with quantities 12, 7, and 40 in a short story about a Saturday soccer tournament. Show work.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 4, 1, or 17. Teacher checks method + units.\n2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 4, 5, or 36. Teacher checks method + units.\n3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 13, 0, or 15. Teacher checks method + units.\n4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 1, or 42. Teacher checks method + units.\n5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 4, or 21. Teacher checks method + units.\n6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 12, 7, or 40. Teacher checks method + units.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to forgetting that height must be perpendicular…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Area of Rectangles and Parallelograms.” Use one concrete numeric example with 8 and -3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
