    content: "# Shape of a Distribution\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 11 of 11 · Data and Statistics · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** describe symmetric, skewed, and clustered shapes.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a science fair supply run: 7 and 3. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 11 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Shape of a Distribution** — sits inside data and statistics. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a science fair supply run. Related quantities show up as 7 and 3 (and sometimes a third measure 10).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and 3. One useful related value is 10; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to 6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a science fair supply run?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to 3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 23, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Shape of a Distribution” but switch the context to a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Use numbers 5, 5, and 10.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** plotting (y, x) instead of (x, y) on the coordinate plane.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and 3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and 3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 5 and 10. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a science fair supply run).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “21.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 5, 9, and 43 in a short story about a band concert ticket table. Show work.\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 9, 11, and 26 in a short story about a school garden plot. Show work.\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 9, 6, and 45 in a short story about a family road trip on I-20. Show work.\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 14, 11, and 20 in a short story about a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Show work.\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 14, 14, and 39 in a short story about an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Show work.\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Shape of a Distribution” with quantities 9, 16, and 22 in a short story about a Prosper Prep basketball practice. Show work.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 5, 9, or 43. Teacher checks method + units.\n2. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 9, 11, or 26. Teacher checks method + units.\n3. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 9, 6, or 45. Teacher checks method + units.\n4. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 11, or 20. Teacher checks method + units.\n5. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 14, 14, or 39. Teacher checks method + units.\n6. Use the taught procedure; expect a reasoned numeric or simplified-ratio/expression answer involving 9, 16, or 22. Teacher checks method + units.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Shape of a Distribution” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to plotting (y, x) instead of (x,…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Shape of a Distribution.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and 3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
